# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EXAMPLE ONLY — pointer file. Nothing here is loaded by the application.
#
# This file deliberately does NOT duplicate the environment reference. Keeping a
# second copy of the key list guarantees the two drift apart.
#
# The authoritative templates are:
#
#   backend/.env.example   →  copy to  PROJECT_PATH/backend/.env    (the backend reads this)
#   .env.example           →  repository-root overview of every key, backend and frontend
#
# See also: docs/deployment-templates.md and deployment/README.md
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Per-instance deployment notes — what to change after copying backend/.env.example:
#
#   APP_ENV=production
#       Not "development".
#   PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://CLIENT_DOMAIN
#       The canonical HTTPS storefront origin, with no path, query or fragment.
#
#   SECRET_KEY
#       Unique per instance. Generate, never reuse between clients, never commit:
#           python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
#       Rotating it invalidates every issued admin token, which is the intended
#       way to force all administrators to sign in again.
#
#   DATABASE_URL
#       This instance's own database and database user. One database per client.
#       SQLite is intended for local development; a server deployment should use
#       MySQL 8 — see docs/future-mysql-migration.md.
#
#   CORS_ORIGINS
#       Comma-separated, no spaces required, e.g. https://CLIENT_DOMAIN
#       A JSON list is also accepted. Do not use "*" — admin calls are authenticated.
#
#   LOCAL_MEDIA_ROOT
#       A directory the service user can write to, matching the Nginx `alias` in
#       nginx/commerce-CLIENT_SLUG.conf.example. It must be listed in the systemd
#       unit's ReadWritePaths.
#
#   LOCAL_MEDIA_BASE_URL=/media
#       Served by Nginx directly from disk, not through the API.
#
#   MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE_BYTES
#       Keep Nginx's client_max_body_size at or slightly above this, so the
#       rejection comes from the application with a proper error shape.
#
#   R2_*
#       Leave empty. The Cloudflare R2 adapter exists but is inert; selecting it
#       without credentials raises a configuration error. See docs/future-r2-integration.md.
#
#   INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL / INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD
#       Leave empty in the deployed file. Create the administrator explicitly:
#           python -m app.initial_data --email <address> --password '<generated>'
#       An unconfigured instance has no usable admin account at all, which is the
#       intended default.
#
# File permissions on the deployed .env:
#
#   chown commerce-CLIENT_SLUG:commerce-CLIENT_SLUG PROJECT_PATH/backend/.env
#   chmod 600 PROJECT_PATH/backend/.env
#
# Never commit a real .env. The repository .gitignore already excludes it; confirm with:
#
#   git check-ignore -v backend/.env
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
